import std/io

// A map destructuring whose key is ABSENT — the one place this construct
// produces observable output of its own.
//
// It is a TRAP and not an assertion failure, and that is the distinction worth
// pinning: `assert {"a" => v} = m` would report through the assertion channel,
// while `{"a" => v} = m` faults with
// `line N: key K not found in map`. The two exits are different and the
// statement forms sit next to each other in stmtInto, so nothing but a test
// keeps them apart.
//
// The key is rendered by `Display`, not by `Debug`, so a String key prints
// bare with no quotes. A path that reached for the inspect spelling would
// produce `"z"` here and agree on everything else.
//
// The missing key is a SIDE-EFFECTING EXPRESSION, and that is the second thing
// this fixture pins. The fault text needs the key rendered, so the key is read
// twice — once by the lookup and once by the report — and a lowering that
// spelled the expression at both sites would print `evaluating z` twice where
// it should print once. That is invisible on any destructuring that hits,
// because the report lives inside the miss branch, so it can only be caught
// here.
//
// The run must produce the partial stdout, the error text and exit 1 that the
// golden records, and the text has one implementation (rt.MapKeyMissingError).
fn main() {
    m = {"a" => 1, "b" => 2}
    {note("a") => present} = m
    io.print("present = ${present}")
    {note("z") => absent} = m
    io.print("unreachable ${absent}")
}

fn note(s: String): String {
    io.print("evaluating ${s}")
    s
}
